 [17592186481293 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481293 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481293 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481293 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481293 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481293 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186480779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481293 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "960a006f-092b-5cc0-ae1b-106bba927b26" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481296 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481296 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481296 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481172 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481296 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481296 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481296 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481296 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481296 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186481213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481296 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a2bf78da-ab1c-5f71-b728-dc9a284a8f6d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481299 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481299 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481299 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481299 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481299 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481299 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481299 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481299 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186481087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481299 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ad0272b4-dd5a-544a-a7d4-a49f4c49e16a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481311 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481311 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481311 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481311 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481311 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481311 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481311 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481311 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186480919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481311 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c47eb18a-bfe5-5e49-bb9e-a97fe694f936" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481318 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481318 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481318 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481318 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481318 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481318 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481318 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481318 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186481209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481318 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d52b8610-a26d-566a-a9f0-2b005b0ef722" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481349 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481349 #edb/ref 1069 "q-146615" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481349 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481349 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(4,1)=0.77,\\,F(4,-1)=0.15,\\,F(1,1)=0.19,\\,F(1,-1)=0.09,\n$$\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{0,1,2,3,4,5\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(1<X\\le 4,-1<Y\\le 1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481349 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1374041d-2f69-55e3-9b42-ce863bb1bab0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481349 #edb/ref 1074 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{0,1,2,3,4,5\\}\\times \\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support.\n\n![](images/01/01e04dfdac5f4abc16bd856dcfbe1b7361010cce2e7779658a33f293c1c52ae4.png)\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/2d/2d2525118ca3b62c0829c0308fab168d0b806dfa13302c007a63cd354314d63f.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(4,1).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $(4,-1)$ and $(1,1).$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to add back the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(1,-1).$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1<X\\le 4,-1<Y\\le 1) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,-1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le -1)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(1,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 1,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(1,-1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 1,Y\\le -1)}} \\\\  & =F(4,1)-F(4,-1)-F(1,1)+F(1,-1) \\\\  & =0.77-0.15-0.19+0.09 \\\\  & =0.52.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481359 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481359 #edb/ref 1069 "q-146338" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481359 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481359 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.35$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.25$ | $0.15$ |\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(2,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481359 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "40beaebe-e39b-5263-8664-2ca0bd457e93" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481359 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nBy definition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2,0) & =P(X\\le 2,Y\\le 0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by highlighting all cells in the table that correspond to $X\\le 2,Y\\le 0.$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.35$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.25$ | $0.15$ |\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2,0) & ={\\sum }_{x\\le 2}{\\sum }_{y\\le 0}f(x,y) \\\\  & =f(1,0)+f(2,0) \\\\  & =0.35+0.25 \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481369 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481369 #edb/ref 1069 "q-146409" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481369 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481369 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{0,1\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{1,2,3,4,5,6\\}.\n$$\n\nFind an expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x>1,y>6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481369 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5555465c-a779-5eab-9f3a-3128b4e5cfe0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481369 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{0,1\\}\\times \\{1,2,3,4,5,6\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x>1$ and $y>6,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/64/644db930cdb0d683a0bcae61b2596dbaeaa4c46df45d8347053c742d2ded3af9.png)\n\nSince every value in the support of $X$ is at most $1$ and $x>1,$ every possible value of $X$ satisfies $X\\le x.$ Similarly, every value in the support of $Y$ is at most $6$ and $y>6,$ so every possible value of $Y$ satisfies $Y\\le y.$\n\nTherefore, the region $Δ$ contains the entire joint support, and\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)=1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481372 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481372 #edb/ref 1069 "q-146624" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481372 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481372 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(4,3)=0.8,\\,F(1,3)=0,\\,F(4,1)=0.3,\n$$\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{2,4,6\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{0,1,2,3,4\\}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le 4,1<Y\\le 3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481372 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5e2590f0-16cb-5122-9f2a-082c6b4b3553" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481372 #edb/ref 1074 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{2,4,6\\}\\times \\{0,1,2,3,4\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support.\n\n![](images/2b/2b159f4ba1873270f42b7afd2fdc4b969672c7d3cb7e694d519ab82dab33b283.png)\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/e1/e186cf0e38e938798cdcd5bc10a5334230cc78cc77d114a14eba0c07702a1cdc.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(4,3)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(4,1)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/eb/ebcfd8e0b553cab38a76eac77aab45186db92b3fe47a1fbb87a8623db4c29389.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 4,1<Y\\le 3) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,3)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le 3)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & =F(4,3)-F(4,1) \\\\  & =0.8-0.3 \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481377 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481377 #edb/ref 1069 "q-146413" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481377 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481377 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{2,3,4\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{2,3,4\\}.\n$$\n\nFind an expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $2<x<4,y<2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481377 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "65aabd4c-4895-5cab-b402-d575edfca45d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481377 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{2,3,4\\}\\times \\{2,3,4\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $2<x<4$ and $y<2,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/4c/4cfff97ef922ffe99f880771d525d616e6c5b601406597813d63425e5ab19345.png)\n\nSince every value in the support of $Y$ is at least $2$ and $y<2,$ no possible value of $Y$ satisfies $Y\\le y.$ Therefore, there are no points in the joint support that lie in the region $Δ.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481393 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481393 #edb/ref 1069 "q-146851" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481393 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481393 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(5,3)=0.6,\\,F(5,1)=0.23,\\,F(2,3)=0.32,\\,F(2,1)=0.05,\n$$\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{1,2,3,4,5,6\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{1,2,3,4\\}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(2<X\\le 5,2\\le Y<4).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481393 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8dece31e-c114-5d98-b9c8-904ef5ba8d5d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481393 #edb/ref 1074 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{1,2,3,4,5,6\\}\\times \\{1,2,3,4\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's plot the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support, and sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/2d/2dd2929b39c9d0357516ebea9f1f31ed2b5f64c6154184ec904996c51162c144.png)\n\nNotice that this region encompasses the same possible support points as the rectangular region with its top-right corner at $(5,3)$ and bottom-left corner at $(2,1)$ below, where the top and right boundaries are inclusive and the bottom and left boundaries are exclusive.\n\n![](images/db/db601c0b0d2931a61d2218e196ea7485fc8d258cb77c604dc44661687de43beb.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(5,3).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $(5,1)$ and $(2,3).$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to add back the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(2,1).$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<X\\le 5,2\\le Y<4) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(5,3)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 3)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(5,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 1)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(2,3)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 2,Y\\le 3)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(2,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 2,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & =F(5,3)-F(5,1)-F(2,3)+F(2,1) \\\\  & =0.6-0.23-0.32+0.05 \\\\  & =0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481415 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481415 #edb/ref 1069 "q-146389" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481415 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481415 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ |\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(2,4).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481415 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c34fa369-1de0-5740-997b-e34e018ecb92" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481415 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nBy definition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2,4) & =P(X\\le 2,Y\\le 4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by highlighting all cells in the table that correspond to $X\\le 2,Y\\le 4.$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ |\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2,4) & ={\\sum }_{x\\le 2}{\\sum }_{y\\le 4}f(x,y) \\\\  & =f(1,1)+f(1,2)+f(1,3) \\\\  & =0.2+0.3+0.1 \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481420 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481420 #edb/ref 1069 "q-146849" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481420 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481420 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(1,1)=0.7,\\,F(1,-2)=0.3,\\,F(-2,1)=0.4,\\,F(-2,-2)=0.1,\n$$\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(-1.5\\le X\\le 1.5,-1.5\\le Y\\le 1.5).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481420 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d10653a4-2ac5-5be4-9b7e-7dbc6ddafa84" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481420 #edb/ref 1074 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}\\times \\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's plot the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support, and sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/ae/ae7d480e52257825e679349ce185b4aa08ed3b096fd7a780ece56c90439798fe.png)\n\nNotice that this region encompasses the same possible support points as the rectangular region with its top-right corner at $(1,1)$ and bottom-left corner at $(-2,-2)$ below, where the top and right boundaries are inclusive and the bottom and left boundaries are exclusive.\n\n![](images/5b/5bcb1b0b518485dfcd4066a0259fea4a2dc050be47e95b4de0b925d8f1c997aa.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(1,1).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $(1,-2)$ and $(-2,1).$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to add back the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(-2,-2).$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.5\\le X\\le 1.5,-1.5\\le Y\\le 1.5) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(1,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 1,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(1,-2)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 1,Y\\le -2)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(-2,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le -2,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(-2,-2)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le -2,Y\\le -2)}} \\\\  & =F(1,1)-F(1,-2)-F(-2,1)+F(-2,-2) \\\\  & =0.7-0.3-0.4+0.1 \\\\  & =0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481437 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481437 #edb/ref 1069 "q-146620" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481437 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481437 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(15,6)=0.82\\,F(12,6)=0.6\\,F(6,6)=0.12,\n$$\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{3,6,9,12,15\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{2,4,6,8,10\\}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(6<X\\le 12,Y\\le 6).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481437 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fab237a1-fe76-5f6d-bb1c-9924690227b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481437 #edb/ref 1074 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{3,6,9,12,15\\}\\times \\{2,4,6,8,10\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support.\n\n![](images/71/715408bbd41d8551b8eadce9df6259f763ec34a702d48f4d116c4164ae5fda50.png)\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/2c/2c4dbaa771e897551b72f0c12ad2ba6940dfbef450c89815dc0f8912babd6a05.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(12,6)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(6,6)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/fb/fb0624aa6e88d4811e77d3c38f24d1d20d201b8e7f6cfb23a3278afef794ab5d.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(6<X\\le 12,Y\\le 6) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(12,6)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 12,Y\\le 6)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(6,6)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 6,Y\\le 6)}} \\\\  & =F(12,6)-F(6,6) \\\\  & =0.6-0.12 \\\\  & =0.48.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481438 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481438 #edb/ref 1069 "q-146845" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481438 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481438 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(5,1)=0.67,\\,F(3,1)=0.43\\,F(1,1)=0.2,\n$$\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{1,2,3,4,5\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(X>3,Y<2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481438 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fd922824-164f-5af2-954c-b939958ff429" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481438 #edb/ref 1074 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{1,2,3,4,5\\}\\times \\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's plot the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support, and sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/cd/cdb5c8084958ba3dc1175c1e92035d89fb4edcdf8ce333962b49f22b40552f71.png)\n\nNotice that this region encompasses the same possible support points as the infinite rectangle with its top-right corner at $(5,1)$ and top-left corner at $(3,1),$ where the top and right boundaries are included, and the left boundary is excluded.\n\n![](images/1c/1c28f3e2f820511a30aa64ccd3affacaa6d72e69da9c596827429b244bf1129a.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(5,1)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(3,1)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/ae/ae993ae6e8c0d71a42ca0e11ed5b30a8d762b3fab12771247eca262f0131c4f5.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>3,Y<2) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(5,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 1)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(3,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 3,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & =F(5,1)-F(3,1) \\\\  & =0.67-0.43 \\\\  & =0.24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494308 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186495195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494308 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186495199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494308 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186495221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494308 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186495222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494308 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186495230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494308 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494308 #edb/ref 1045 "The Joint CDF of Two Discrete Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494308 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186495222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494308 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494308 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494308 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "8dcc3cb1-af1f-5fd5-a39e-26295bdafc56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494383 #edb/ref 1080 "0.4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494383 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494383 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "213c512b-4d83-52c6-aea3-4aa799b73e2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494449 #edb/ref 1080 "0.1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494449 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494449 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3ee2c542-f613-5ede-8ccb-305ddcfbc168" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494481 #edb/ref 1080 "0.3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494481 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494481 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5226bb01-1d74-50d5-ae38-837109e0e401" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494483 #edb/ref 1080 "0.2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17