#edb/ref 17592186519268 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519488 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186519309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519488 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186519398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519488 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519488 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519488 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186519398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519488 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c166ee4f-8c2c-5d38-86bd-25689b657099" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519518 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519518 #edb/ref 1069 "q-146341" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519518 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519518 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ |\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(2,3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519518 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "097fc2ae-2fef-58ac-bba4-f69c8c04dea2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519518 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nBy definition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2,3) & =P(X\\le 2,Y\\le 3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by highlighting all cells in the table that correspond to $X\\le 2,Y\\le 3.$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ |\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2,3) & ={\\sum }_{x\\le 2}{\\sum }_{y\\le 3}f(x,y) \\\\  & =f(1,1)+f(1,2)+f(1,3)+f(2,1)+f(2,2)+f(2,3) \\\\  & =0.1+0.2+0.2+0.15+0.1+0.1 \\\\  & =0.85.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519537 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519479 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519537 #edb/ref 1069 "q-146466" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519537 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519537 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(12,6)=0.81,\\,F(12,4)=0.3,\\,F(6,6)=0.17,\\,F(6,4)=0.1,\n$$\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{3,6,9,12,15\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{2,4,6,8,10\\}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(6<X\\le 12,4<Y\\le 6).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519537 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "45f60a39-7c3b-515c-9608-b1c0fb2b6cd7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519537 #edb/ref 1074 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{3,6,9,12,15\\}\\times \\{2,4,6,8,10\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support.\n\n![](images/71/715408bbd41d8551b8eadce9df6259f763ec34a702d48f4d116c4164ae5fda50.png)\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/5c/5c50878b85d26a87d6220a99f6fbe896925a6802801117239a5ba56dcba46906.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(12,6).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $(12,4)$ and $(6,6).$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to add back the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(6,4).$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(6<X\\le 12,4<Y\\le 6) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(12,6)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 12,Y\\le 6)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(12,4)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 12,Y\\le 4)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(6,6)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 6,Y\\le 6)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(6,4)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 6,Y\\le 4)}} \\\\  & =F(12,6)-F(12,4)-F(6,6)+F(6,4) \\\\  & =0.81-0.3-0.17+0.1 \\\\  & =0.44.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519541 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519541 #edb/ref 1069 "q-146465" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519541 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519541 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(7,10)=0.82,\\,F(7,4)=0.11,\\,F(7,2)=0.05,\n$$\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{1,3,5,7,9\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{2,4,6,8,10\\}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le 7,4<Y\\le 10).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519541 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "560539a1-a567-51bf-978e-83db525442bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519541 #edb/ref 1074 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{1,3,5,7,9\\}\\times \\{2,4,6,8,10\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support.\n\n![](images/9e/9e7509e29912cc54dde9a86786f1d171a49b89e7b5f7305ef025582b3402350a.png)\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/78/78a130ca07be1acecc4655a557abb8d3d037da28f143d0696272d12898b6b4cd.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(7,10)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(7,4)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/e4/e4ff1e7cd8259fa9bcaae75210a7a3f2aeeb547ceba46b2fb411f94133372fa2.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 7,4<Y\\le 10) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(7,10)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 7,Y\\le 10)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(7,4)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 7,Y\\le 4)}} \\\\  & =F(7,10)-F(7,4) \\\\  & =0.82-0.11 \\\\  & =0.71.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519552 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519552 #edb/ref 1069 "q-146408" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519552 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519552 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{0,2,4,6\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{0,1,3\\}.\n$$\n\nFind an expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x>6,y>3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519552 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "741ee6eb-9658-524e-8fcb-9b428519f04d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519552 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{0,2,4,6\\}\\times \\{0,1,3\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x>6$ and $y>3,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/63/6340ca35646830558631c7fe523698a201c5acf26d1360910ab207c30f92e41f.png)\n\nSince every value in the support of $X$ is at most $6$ and $x>6,$ every possible value of $X$ satisfies $X\\le x.$ Similarly, every value in the support of $Y$ is at most $3$ and $y>3,$ so every possible value of $Y$ satisfies $Y\\le y.$\n\nTherefore, the region $Δ$ contains the entire joint support, and\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)=1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519565 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519565 #edb/ref 1069 "q-146394" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519565 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519565 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=-1$ | $0.15$ | $0.25$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(0,\\frac{3}{2}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519565 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9adae9aa-2d5a-51d9-80a6-fbe056bdc031" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519565 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nBy definition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0,\\frac{3}{2}) & =P(X\\le 0,Y\\le \\frac{3}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by highlighting all cells in the table that correspond to $X\\le 0,Y\\le \\frac{3}{2}.$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=-1$ | $0.15$ | $0.25$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0,\\frac{3}{2}) & ={\\sum }_{x\\le 0}{\\sum }_{y\\le 3/2}f(x,y) \\\\  & =f(-1,-1)+f(-1,0)+f(-1,1) \\\\  & =0.15+0.25+0.05 \\\\  & =0.45.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519571 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519571 #edb/ref 1069 "q-146411" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519571 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519571 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{1,2,3\\}.\n$$\n\nFind an expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x<2,y<1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519571 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a2622f87-4a36-506a-86f1-c353a2d6ebaa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519571 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}\\times \\{1,2,3\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x<2$ and $y<1,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/39/3911ef3923eabce680eb406a93c02aaacd2b91f829294eb7ac0e26a11753a703.png)\n\nSince every value in the support of $Y$ is at least $1$ and $y<1,$ no possible value of $Y$ satisfies $Y\\le y.$ Therefore, there are no points in the joint support that lie in the region $Δ.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
